      "side": 1,
      "message": "`always()` keeps this job running when a newer patch set cancels the run.\n\nWhen GitHub cancels a run, it re-checks each job\u0027s `if`, and `always()` stays true, so this job isn\u0027t cancelled. If it hasn\u0027t started yet, it starts once `verify` is cancelled. (The vote job relies on the same behaviour, which is why the Worker handles a `cancelled` result.) So when a patch set is replaced while it builds, the old run still mints a token, runs a full `xhigh` review of up to 200 steps, and posts it with a vote on the replaced patch set. The cancelled run stays in progress until this job ends, so the new patch set\u0027s run should wait behind it in the `gerrit-\u003cchange\u003e` group, for up to 45 minutes. It will happen often: this change\u0027s PS2 came four minutes after PS1.\n\nFix: `!cancelled()` in place of `always()`. A patch set whose build failed still gets reviewed.",
      "revId": "6857a890e77f8ff3236f7458319e74d1d7d24579",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
