      "writtenOn": "2026-11-20T22:40:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This step runs a file the change\u0027s code can rewrite, and it can still get an OIDC token.\n\nThe skill has Muse build and test the change (`cargo build -p vali-cli`, `cargo test`), so the change\u0027s `build.rs` files and tests run in Muse\u0027s sandbox. The sandbox can write the workspace (line 209). It has to, for the skill\u0027s `target` symlink to work. Only the Muse step blanks `ACTIONS_ID_TOKEN_REQUEST_*` (line 332), so this step still has both, and `TICKET` from the job\u0027s `env`.\n\nSo a patch set can add a `build.rs` that overwrites `.github/review/render-session.py`. The workspace is reachable from `OUT_DIR`, which is under the workspace\u0027s `target/`. Then this step runs the new script, which does what the vote job does: it gets an OIDC token for `gerrithub-ci` and POSTs `/review` with the ticket. That posts any text it likes, with Code-Review +1, as the CI account. Or it POSTs `/muse-token` and uses the LLM key through `/v1` until the run ends, which it can hold off for the job\u0027s 45 minutes. No hostile `build.rs` is needed if Muse gets round SKILL.md:48 by checking out `REV` in the working tree: this step then runs the change\u0027s own copy of the script.\n\nA trusted reviewer\u0027s \"recheck\" can bring anyone\u0027s patch set here. That is more than \"the worst a hostile one can do is steer its review\", and it breaks the header\u0027s rule that the jobs that run the change\u0027s code get no token.\n\nFix: anything that can get an OIDC token must not run a file the sandbox could have written. For example, upload `review.json` and the session export as artifacts, and render and post in a job of their own that `needs: review`, checks out main afresh and has `id-token: write`. Then blank `ACTIONS_ID_TOKEN_REQUEST_*` on every review-job step after the token step. Copying the script to `$RUNNER_TEMP` before Muse and blanking the variables here also works. Post would still run `jq` and `curl` from a `PATH` that has `~/.local/bin` and `~/.cargo/bin` ahead of `/usr/bin`, so check that the sandbox can\u0027t write there.",
      "revId": "6857a890e77f8ff3236f7458319e74d1d7d24579",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
    },
